this is an om stone or ogham stone  depending on whose pronunciation you  follow the carvings on here are from an  alphabet unlike anything else in the  world an alphabet that is literally an  exception to modern rules I'm not  talking about the Roman letters on the  face I'm talking about the markings  carved into the corner those markings  are in om it's a way of writing down the  early Irish language with marks like  these there are only about 400 surviving  stones like this found in Ireland and  the western parts of the UK this  particular one is about 1500 years old  and it comes from Devon in the southwest  of England it's on display here in the  British Museum the inscription is a  named ohm stones are mostly used to  record names either as a tombstone or as  a marker of land ownership you read this  along the stem line along the corner  each character is made of 1 to 5  markings which will all be on one side  of the line the other side of the mine  through the line or on the line when  modern scholars start to analyze this  script they want to write it down on  paper and they adapted it a little to  make it easier for them they changed it  so it always went left to right and each  phrase was drawn on a horizontal line  see if it easily tell which marks were  on which side some arm inscriptions on  latest stones or on other artifacts do  actually carve their own stem line into  a flat surface so adding that line in  print wasn't too much of a stretch and  after all trying to fold a bit of paper  and sketch markings on the corner  wouldn't be easy to work with rakha  Demick papers and that left us years  later with an interesting technology  problem when it came time to encode own  characters as ones and zeros to fold  them into unicode the international  standard for how to display text on a  computer  Owen became the only language in Unicode  where a space is not a space a space  character to a computer has three  properties it has a certain width you  don't display or print anything in that  width and if your text has run out of  room on a line you can go back to the  previous space character and replace it  with a line break now there have been  well understood variations on those for  years two of those rules are flattered  all the time you can have a non-breaking  space I like the one between a word and  a french quotation mark that space has  width it has nothing displayed in it but  you can't put a line break there the  word in the punctuation must travel  together you can also have a zero width  space which sounds like  ridiculous idea but it's a good way to  tell a computer that if there isn't room  it's okay to break a long string of  characters somewhere that it otherwise  wouldn't these are all commonly used but  until Olin was added to Unicode the rule  that a space character must be empty had  never been broken why would it's it's a  space well an arm space includes that  stem line the line doesn't stop between  words because the corner doesn't stop  between words the space is not a space  but it behaves like one it can be  replaced with a line break if you've  spread an om inscription over two lines  the space character vanishes same as in  English now om isn't the only language  that uses a separator like this ancient  Latin uses an inter pact a middle dot  the same way but in modern usage that is  not a space and modern usage wings om is  the only case where modern folks have  gone yeah okay it's a space that also  involves drawing something it's a space  that isn't a space there's been an  actual argument about it down in one of  the mailing lists for linguists and  computer science nerds at the Unicode  consortium the Irish contingent had some  very strong opinions and the final  ruling yeah it's a space that's also a  line this is one of the things that I  love about linguistics an ancient script  carved into stones more than a  millennium ago is an exception to a rule  that I never even realized was there a  space doesn't have to be a space  you
 

